    <statement><p>Suppose the angle <m>\theta</m> is in the fourth quadrant, 
<m>\frac{3\pi}{2} \leq \theta \leq 2\pi</m>, and 
<m>\displaystyle \tan(\theta) = \frac{-2}{3}.</m>  Find 
exact values (as fractions, not decimal approximations) 
for the following.</p>
<p>(a)  <m>\sec(\theta)</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>(b)  <m>\sin(\theta)</m> = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\sqrt{{\frac{13}{9}}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{-2}{3\sqrt{{\frac{13}{9}}}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p><alert>SOLUTION</alert></p>
<p>(a) We begin by using the identity <m>1 + \tan^2(\theta) = \sec^2(\theta)</m> 
to find <m>\sec(\theta).</m></p>
<p><me>\begin{array}{rcl} 
1 + \tan^2(\theta) \amp  = \amp  \sec^2(\theta) 
\\[1ex] 
1 + \left( \frac{-2}{3} \right)^2 \amp  = \amp  \sec^2(\theta) 
\\[1ex] 
\pm \sqrt{ \frac{13}{9} } \amp  = \amp  \sec(\theta). 
\end{array}</me></p>
<p>Since <m>\frac{3\pi}{2} \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>, 
<m>\sec(\theta ) = \frac{1}{\cos(\theta)} \ge 0</m>, and thus
<m>\sec(\theta) = \sqrt{ \frac{13}{9} } = \frac{\sqrt{13}}{3}.</m></p>
<p>(b) Since <m>\tan(\theta) = \frac{ \sin(\theta) }{ \cos(\theta) } 
= \sin(\theta) \sec(\theta)</m>, we have</p>
<p><me>\sin(\theta) 
= \frac{ \tan(\theta) }{ \sec(\theta) } 
= \frac{ -2/3 }{ \sqrt{13} / 3 } 
= \frac{-2}{\sqrt{13}}.</me></p>
</solution>
